/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/data-plus.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("data-plus", [["dp1",0,1,"A marketing team collects customer feedback as free-text emails, recorded support calls and product photos. How is this data best classified?",["Structured","Semi-structured","Unstructured","Relational"],2,"Free text, audio and images have no predefined data model, so they are unstructured. Semi-structured data such as JSON has keys or tags that give it some shape, which these files lack.","Domain 1",["Structured data fits a fixed schema of rows and columns; free text, audio and photos have no such schema.","Semi-structured data such as JSON carries keys or tags; plain emails, recordings and photos do not describe their contents that way.",null,"Relational describes a database model built from related tables, not a category for raw text, audio and images."],1],["dp2",0,1,"An e-commerce API returns orders as JSON. Each order has nested line items, and some orders include optional fields that others do not. How is this data classified?",["Semi-structured","Unstructured","Structured","Binary large object"],0,"JSON carries keys that describe the data but allows nesting and optional fields, which makes it semi-structured. Structured data has a fixed tabular schema, which this does not.","Domain 1",[null,"Unstructured data has no model at all, but JSON has keys that label every value, so it is not unstructured.","Structured data needs every record to share one fixed schema; nested items and optional fields break that rule.","A binary large object is a database type for opaque binary content such as images, not a classification for readable JSON."],1],["dp3",0,1,"In a sales database, each Customers row has a unique, non-null CustomerID. The Orders table stores the CustomerID of the customer who placed each order. What is CustomerID in the Orders table?",["Primary key","Foreign key","Composite key","Candidate key"],1,"A foreign key is a column that refers to the primary key of another table and links the two. CustomerID is the primary key only in Customers; in Orders many rows can share the same value.","Domain 1",["CustomerID is the primary key only in Customers; in Orders it repeats for every order the customer places.",null,"A composite key combines two or more columns to identify a row; CustomerID here is a single column pointing elsewhere.","A candidate key is a column that could uniquely identify rows in its own table, but CustomerID repeats in Orders."],1],["dp4",0,1,"A social app must store users and who follows whom, and quickly answer questions such as 'which friends of my friends like this page?' Which database type fits best?",["Key-value store","Column-family store","Relational data mart","Graph database"],3,"Graph databases store nodes and relationships directly, so multi-hop relationship queries are fast. A key-value store only looks up values by key and cannot traverse relationships efficiently.","Domain 1",["A key-value store fetches a value by its key only, so it cannot efficiently follow chains of relationships.","Column-family stores are built for massive write volumes such as time series, not multi-hop relationship queries.","A relational data mart would need repeated self-joins for friends-of-friends queries, which get slow as hops increase.",null],2],["dp5",0,1,"After importing a customer file, ZIP codes such as 02134 appear as 2134. What should the analyst change?",["Import the ZIP column as text","Format the column as currency","Convert the column to a date type","Store the column as a decimal"],0,"ZIP codes are identifiers, not quantities, so they belong in a text (string) type that keeps leading zeros. Any numeric type, including decimal, drops the leading zero.","Domain 1",[null,"Currency formatting still treats ZIP codes as numbers, so the leading zero stays lost and a currency symbol is added.","ZIP codes are not dates; converting them to a date type would produce errors or meaningless values.","A decimal is still a numeric type, so it drops the leading zero just as an integer does."],1],["dp6",0,1,"A team stores billions of rows for analytics and wants a compressed, column-oriented file format so queries that read only a few columns scan less data. Which format fits?",["CSV","Parquet","XML","TSV"],1,"Parquet is a columnar, compressed format built for analytic workloads, so a query can read just the columns it needs. CSV and TSV are row-oriented plain text and must be read in full.","Domain 1",["CSV is row-oriented plain text with no compression or stored types, so every query reads whole rows.",null,"XML is verbose, tag-based text that is row-oriented and uncompressed, the opposite of what large analytics needs.","TSV is CSV with tab delimiters; it has the same row-oriented, uncompressed limitations."],2],["dp7",0,1,"A company wants one central repository of cleaned, integrated historical data from sales, finance and HR, modeled for reporting and BI. What should it build?",["A data lake","An OLTP database","A data warehouse","A flat file share"],2,"A data warehouse integrates cleaned, structured data from many sources and models it for analysis. A data lake keeps raw data in native formats, and an OLTP database is built for day-to-day transactions.","Domain 1",["A data lake keeps raw data in native formats; the requirement here is cleaned, integrated data modeled for BI.","An OLTP database runs day-to-day transactions and is not designed to integrate history from many systems for reporting.",null,"A flat file share holds loose files with no integration, modeling or query engine for enterprise reporting."],1],["dp8",0,1,"A data science team wants to land raw application logs, images and JSON files cheaply in their native formats and decide on structure only when they read the data. What should they use?",["A data lake","A data mart","A data warehouse","An OLTP database"],0,"A data lake stores raw data of any type and applies schema on read. Warehouses and data marts apply a schema on write and expect cleaned, structured data.","Domain 1",[null,"A data mart is a cleaned, subject-focused subset with a schema applied on load, not a store for raw native files.","A data warehouse applies a schema on write and expects cleaned, structured data rather than raw images and logs.","An OLTP database is built for transactional records, not cheap storage of raw logs, images and JSON files."],1],["dp9",0,1,"Which statement correctly contrasts OLTP and OLAP systems?",["OLTP stores only unstructured data; OLAP stores only structured data","OLTP always runs in the cloud; OLAP always runs on premises","OLTP powers dashboards; OLAP records point-of-sale transactions","OLTP handles many small writes; OLAP runs large read-heavy analytic queries"],3,"OLTP systems process many short inserts and updates to run the business, while OLAP systems run complex queries over history to analyze it. The dashboard option reverses the two roles.","Domain 1",["Both OLTP and OLAP systems usually hold structured data; the difference is the workload, not the data shape.","Either kind of system can run in the cloud or on premises; hosting location does not define them.","This reverses the roles: point-of-sale transactions are OLTP work and dashboards are typically served by OLAP.",null],2],["dp10",0,1,"A retail star schema has Date, Product and Store dimensions. Which table should hold the quantity sold and the sales amount for each transaction line?",["The Product dimension","The Date dimension","The fact table","The Store dimension"],2,"Measures such as quantity and amount live in the fact table, along with foreign keys to each dimension. Dimensions hold descriptive attributes used to filter and group those measures.","Domain 1",["The Product dimension holds descriptive attributes such as name and category, not per-transaction measures.","The Date dimension describes calendar attributes such as month and quarter, not quantities sold.",null,"The Store dimension describes store attributes such as location, not the amounts of each sale."],1],["dp11",0,1,"A customer moves to a new city. The warehouse must keep the old city on past sales and add a new row with effective dates for future sales. Which technique is this?",["Type 1 slowly changing dimension","Type 2 slowly changing dimension","Snowflake schema","Degenerate dimension"],1,"A Type 2 slowly changing dimension adds a new row (with effective dates or a current flag) so history is preserved. Type 1 simply overwrites the old value and loses history.","Domain 1",["Type 1 overwrites the old value, so past sales would show the new city and history would be lost.",null,"A snowflake schema is about normalizing dimensions into sub-tables, not about tracking attribute changes over time.","A degenerate dimension is a dimension key, such as an invoice number, kept in the fact table with no dimension table."],3],["dp12",0,1,"A designer splits the Product dimension into separate Product, Subcategory and Category tables linked by keys. What kind of schema results?",["Star schema","Flat file schema","Galaxy schema","Snowflake schema"],3,"Normalizing a dimension into related sub-tables turns a star into a snowflake schema. A star keeps each dimension in one denormalized table; a galaxy schema has multiple fact tables sharing dimensions.","Domain 1",["A star schema keeps each dimension in one denormalized table, the opposite of splitting it into sub-tables.","Flat file schema is not a dimensional modeling pattern; the design here is a set of linked tables.","A galaxy schema has several fact tables sharing dimensions, which is not what splitting one dimension creates.",null],2],["dp13",0,1,"An analyst needs to share an analysis in which code, its output charts and explanatory text sit together and can be re-run by a colleague. What should they use?",["A CSV export","A Jupyter notebook","A stored procedure","A pivot table"],1,"Notebooks combine executable code, results and narrative in one document that others can re-run. A stored procedure holds code only, and a CSV holds data only.","Domain 1",["A CSV export holds only data, with no code, charts or narrative that a colleague could re-run.",null,"A stored procedure is saved database code; it does not combine charts and explanatory text for sharing.","A pivot table summarizes data in a spreadsheet but does not hold runnable code alongside narrative."],1],["dp14",0,1,"Which language is designed specifically for querying and managing data in relational databases?",["Python","R","SQL","JavaScript"],2,"SQL (Structured Query Language) is the standard language for relational databases. Python and R are general and statistical languages that can call SQL but are not built for it.","Domain 1",["Python is a general-purpose language that can send SQL to databases but was not designed for querying them.","R was built for statistics and graphics; it can connect to databases but is not their query language.",null,"JavaScript is mainly a web programming language and is not the standard language of relational databases."],1],["dp15",0,1,"A subscription company has three years of customer records labeled 'churned' or 'stayed' and wants to predict which current customers will leave. Which approach fits?",["Robotic process automation","Unsupervised clustering","A generative image model","Supervised machine learning"],3,"Supervised learning trains on examples with known labels to predict the label for new cases. Clustering is unsupervised and groups similar records without labels, so it cannot learn 'churned' directly.","Domain 1",["RPA repeats rule-based user-interface tasks; it does not learn from labeled history to predict churn.","Clustering is unsupervised and groups similar customers without using the churned or stayed labels.","A generative image model creates pictures; it does not predict a yes/no outcome from customer records.",null],2],["dp16",0,1,"Each day a clerk copies values from emailed invoices into the same ERP screen, following fixed rules. Which technology is designed to automate this kind of repetitive, rule-based screen work?",["Robotic process automation","Natural language processing","A data lakehouse","Linear regression"],0,"RPA uses software bots to repeat rule-based actions in user interfaces. NLP analyzes human language, and while it might help read invoice text, it does not by itself perform the data entry.","Domain 1",[null,"NLP could help read invoice text, but it does not itself perform the repetitive clicks and data entry in the ERP screen.","A data lakehouse is a storage architecture, not a tool that automates screen-based tasks.","Linear regression predicts a number from other variables; it does not automate copying values between applications."],1],["dp17",0,1,"An analyst uses a large language model to draft a complex SQL query for a monthly report. What should the analyst do next?",["Run it on the production database right away","Review and test it against known results","Convert the query to Python first","Send the output to stakeholders as final"],1,"LLM output can look correct but contain wrong joins or logic, so it must be reviewed and validated against known figures. Running it straight away or publishing unchecked results risks errors.","Domain 1",["Running unreviewed generated SQL on production risks wrong results and heavy load on a live system.",null,"Translating the query into Python does not verify its logic; the same mistakes can carry over.","Publishing unchecked output risks sending stakeholders wrong numbers that damage trust."],2],["dp18",0,1,"A column stores prices such as 19.99 and 120.50, and totals must match accounting to the cent. Which data type is the best choice?",["Floating point (float)","Integer","Fixed-precision decimal","String"],2,"Fixed-precision DECIMAL or NUMERIC types store exact values, so sums match to the cent. Floating-point types store binary approximations that can create small rounding errors in money totals.","Domain 1",["Floats store binary approximations, so money totals can be off by fractions of a cent.","Integers hold whole numbers only, so 19.99 would be truncated or rounded unless stored as cents.",null,"Storing prices as text prevents arithmetic and sorting by value without converting them first."],2],["dp19",0,2,"A team needs daily exchange rates from a provider that offers authenticated HTTPS endpoints returning JSON. What is the best acquisition method?",["Scrape the provider's HTML pages","Manually copy rates into a spreadsheet","Call the provider's REST API","Ask for a monthly CSV by email"],2,"An API is the provider's supported, structured and automatable way to get the data. Scraping HTML breaks when the page layout changes and may violate the site's terms.","Domain 2",["Scraping HTML is fragile and may breach the provider's terms when a supported API already exists.","Manual copying is slow, error-prone and cannot run reliably every day.",null,"A monthly emailed CSV cannot supply daily rates and adds manual handling to every load."],2],["dp20",0,2,"A company loads raw data from its source systems into a cloud data warehouse first, then cleans and reshapes it with the warehouse's own SQL engine. Which approach is this?",["ELT","ETL","Data masking","OLTP"],0,"In ELT the load happens before the transform, which is done inside the target. In ETL the data is transformed in a separate step before it is loaded.","Domain 2",[null,"In ETL the data is transformed in a separate step before loading, not inside the warehouse afterward.","Data masking hides sensitive values; it is a protection technique, not a load-then-transform pattern.","OLTP describes transactional systems that run the business, not an approach to loading a warehouse."],2],["dp21",0,2,"A nightly job reloads a 50-million-row table, although only a few thousand rows change each day, and it now runs past its time window. Which change helps most?",["Run the full reload twice a night","Load only new or changed rows (incremental load)","Drop the primary key and indexes on the target table","Export to CSV before loading"],1,"An incremental load processes only new or changed rows, often by a modified timestamp or change data capture, which shrinks run time. More full reloads make the problem worse.","Domain 2",["Running the full reload twice doubles the work, so the job would take even longer.",null,"Dropping keys and indexes can speed some bulk loads but still reprocesses all 50 million rows and weakens integrity.","Exporting to CSV adds another step and still moves every row each night."],3],["dp22",0,2,"A fraud team must analyze card transactions within seconds of each purchase. Which pipeline style fits?",["Weekly full reload","Nightly batch ETL","Monthly flat-file export","Streaming ingestion"],3,"Streaming processes events continuously as they arrive, which gives seconds-level latency. Nightly batch would detect fraud only the next day.","Domain 2",["A weekly reload leaves days between a purchase and its analysis, far too slow for fraud checks.","Nightly batch processing means fraud is noticed the next day at the earliest, not within seconds.","A monthly export is weeks behind and useless for blocking fraud as it happens.",null],1],["dp23",0,2,"Which query returns only the regions whose total sales exceed 100000?",["SELECT region, SUM(sales) FROM orders WHERE SUM(sales) > 100000 GROUP BY region;","SELECT region, sales FROM orders WHERE sales > 100000;","SELECT region, SUM(sales) FROM orders ORDER BY region HAVING sales > 100000;","SELECT region, SUM(sales) FROM orders GROUP BY region HAVING SUM(sales) > 100000;"],3,"Conditions on aggregates go in HAVING, after GROUP BY. WHERE filters individual rows before grouping, so SUM() is not allowed there, and filtering sales > 100000 per row answers a different question.","Domain 2",["Aggregates such as SUM cannot be used in WHERE because WHERE filters rows before grouping.","This filters individual orders over 100000 rather than regions whose combined total exceeds it.","HAVING needs GROUP BY and an aggregate condition; this query has neither and puts ORDER BY before HAVING.",null],2],["dp24",0,2,"An analyst needs a list of every customer with their order totals, including customers who have never ordered. Customers is the first (left) table. Which join should be used?",["INNER JOIN","CROSS JOIN","LEFT JOIN","SELF JOIN"],2,"A LEFT JOIN keeps every row from the left table and fills the right-side columns with NULL where there is no match. An INNER JOIN would drop customers without orders.","Domain 2",["An INNER JOIN keeps only customers with matching orders, so customers who never ordered disappear.","A CROSS JOIN pairs every customer with every order, producing a meaningless Cartesian product.",null,"A self join joins a table to itself, such as employees to managers, not customers to orders."],2],["dp25",0,2,"Two monthly extract files have identical columns. The analyst needs one table with every row from both files, keeping any duplicate rows. Which operation fits?",["UNION ALL","INNER JOIN","UNION","GROUP BY"],0,"UNION ALL stacks the rows of both result sets and keeps duplicates. UNION also stacks them but removes duplicate rows, which the analyst does not want here.","Domain 2",[null,"An INNER JOIN combines columns from matching rows side by side; it does not stack the rows of two files.","UNION stacks the rows but removes duplicate rows, which the analyst wants to keep.","GROUP BY collapses rows into groups for aggregation; it does not combine two files."],1],["dp26",0,2,"An INNER JOIN between Orders (1,000 rows) and Customers on CustomerID returns 1,300 rows. What is the most likely cause?",["Some orders have a NULL CustomerID","The query has no ORDER BY clause","The Customers table has duplicate CustomerID values","The Orders table has an index on OrderDate"],2,"If a key appears more than once on the lookup side, each matching order is repeated (fan-out), which inflates row counts and totals. NULL keys would reduce the rows returned, not increase them.","Domain 2",["NULL keys never match in an inner join, so they would reduce the row count, not increase it.","ORDER BY only sorts the result; leaving it out cannot add rows.",null,"An index affects query speed, not how many rows a join returns."],3],["dp27",0,2,"A customer column contains 'ACME Corp', 'Acme Corporation' and 'ACME CORP.' for the same company, so a group-by shows three customers. What kind of data quality issue is this?",["Missing values","Inconsistent values that need standardizing","Statistical outliers in the customer column","Truncated numeric data"],1,"The same entity is recorded in different formats, which is an inconsistency that standardization (and possibly a lookup table) fixes. Nothing is missing, and these are text values, not numeric outliers.","Domain 2",["Nothing is missing: every row has a customer value, just written in different ways.",null,"Outliers are extreme numeric values; these are text variants of the same company name.","Truncation cuts values off at a length limit; these are complete names formatted differently."],1],["dp28",0,2,"In an employee dataset, ages range from 18 to 70, except one record showing 212. What should the analyst do first?",["Delete the Age column","Replace every age with the mean","Check the source and treat it as an invalid value","Keep it, since one row cannot affect results"],2,"212 is impossible for an employee, so it is invalid data; confirm with the source and then correct, exclude or flag it. A single extreme value can distort the mean and standard deviation, so ignoring it is risky.","Domain 2",["Deleting the whole column throws away every valid age because of one bad value.","Replacing every age with the mean destroys all the real variation to fix a single record.",null,"One impossible value can noticeably shift the mean and standard deviation, so it should not be ignored."],2],["dp29",0,2,"In a large survey, about 3% of household income values are missing at random. The analyst wants to keep those rows for other questions. Which approach is most suitable?",["Impute with the median income","Replace the blanks with zero","Delete the income column","Replace the blanks with the maximum income"],0,"Median imputation keeps the rows and is robust to the right skew typical of income data. Zero or the maximum would badly distort the distribution, and deleting the column throws away 97% of good values.","Domain 2",[null,"Zero is not a real income for these households, so it drags the average down and distorts the distribution.","Deleting the column discards the 97% of good values to deal with 3% missing.","Filling blanks with the maximum inflates incomes and badly distorts the distribution."],2],["dp30",0,2,"When imputing missing values, an analyst also adds a column such as income_imputed = 1. Why?",["To make queries run faster","So readers can tell filled-in values from real ones","Because SQL requires a flag column for every NULL value","To encrypt the imputed values"],1,"A flag keeps a record of which values were estimated, so later analysis can test their effect or exclude them. It has nothing to do with performance or encryption, and SQL does not require it.","Domain 2",["A flag column adds data to each row; it does not make queries run faster.",null,"SQL has no such requirement; NULL values can be stored without any companion flag.","A flag marks which values were estimated; it does not encrypt or protect anything."],3],["dp31",0,2,"A Name field holds values like 'Smith, John'. The report needs separate LastName and FirstName columns. Which transformation is needed?",["Concatenation of the two name parts","Binning into name groups","Aggregation by last name","Parsing (splitting) the field"],3,"Parsing splits one string into parts using a delimiter, here the comma. Concatenation is the opposite: it joins fields together.","Domain 2",["Concatenation joins fields into one, which is the opposite of splitting one name into two columns.","Binning groups numeric values into ranges; it does not separate a text field into parts.","Aggregation summarizes many rows into totals; it does not split a single field.",null],1],["dp32",0,2,"Before clustering, an analyst rescales income (thousands to millions) and age (18–90) so both run from 0 to 1. What technique is this?",["Standardization (z-scores)","Binning","Min-max normalization","Unpivoting"],2,"Min-max normalization maps values onto a fixed range, usually 0 to 1. Standardization instead centers values at mean 0 with standard deviation 1, which does not bound them to 0–1.","Domain 2",["Standardization centers values at mean 0 with standard deviation 1, so results are not bounded to 0 to 1.","Binning groups values into ranges or categories rather than rescaling them onto a continuous 0 to 1 scale.",null,"Unpivoting reshapes columns into rows; it does not change the scale of any values."],2],["dp33",0,2,"A report needs exact customer ages converted into groups such as 18–24, 25–34 and 35–44. Which technique is this?",["Binning","Imputation","Parsing","Deduplication"],0,"Binning (discretization) groups continuous values into ranges. Imputation fills in missing values, which is not the goal here.","Domain 2",[null,"Imputation fills in missing values; it does not group existing ages into ranges.","Parsing splits or extracts parts of a string; it does not convert numbers into age bands.","Deduplication removes repeated records; it has nothing to do with grouping ages."],1],["dp34",0,2,"A spreadsheet has one row per product and twelve columns, Jan to Dec, of sales. The BI tool needs one Month column and one Sales column instead. Which operation is needed?",["Pivot","Unpivot (wide to long)","Transpose into a single row","Aggregate to yearly totals"],1,"Unpivoting turns the month columns into rows with an attribute column (Month) and a value column (Sales). Pivoting does the reverse, turning row values into columns.","Domain 2",["Pivoting turns row values into columns, the reverse of what the BI tool needs here.",null,"Transposing flips all rows and columns, producing products as columns rather than a Month and Sales column.","Aggregating to yearly totals loses the monthly detail the BI tool needs."],2],["dp35",0,2,"A report query that filters a 200-million-row table on order_date takes minutes to run. Which change is most likely to help?",["Use SELECT * so the query plan is simpler","Move filtering into the BI tool","Create an index on order_date","Add ORDER BY on every column"],2,"An index on the filtered column lets the database find matching rows without scanning the whole table. Filtering later in the BI tool means all rows are still read and transferred.","Domain 2",["SELECT * reads more columns and does nothing to avoid scanning all 200 million rows.","Filtering in the BI tool means every row is still read and transferred before being discarded.",null,"Sorting by every column adds a large, expensive sort and does not help find the filtered rows."],3],["dp36",0,2,"Why do teams discourage SELECT * in production reporting queries?",["It is not valid in standard SQL","It reads and sends columns that aren't needed","It cannot be combined with a WHERE clause","It locks the table for writing"],1,"SELECT * returns every column, which wastes I/O and network transfer (and cost in column stores) and can break reports when columns are added. It is valid SQL and works with WHERE.","Domain 2",["SELECT * is valid standard SQL; the concern is efficiency and fragility, not syntax.",null,"SELECT * works with WHERE clauses; the column list and row filter are independent.","SELECT * is a read and does not lock the table for writing any more than a named-column query."],2],["dp37",0,2,"A researcher splits customers by region and then randomly samples within each region in proportion to its size. Which sampling method is this?",["Simple random sampling","Convenience sampling","Systematic sampling","Stratified random sampling"],3,"Stratified sampling divides the population into groups (strata) and samples randomly within each, so every group is represented. Systematic sampling picks every nth record from one list.","Domain 2",["Simple random sampling picks from the whole population at once, without first dividing it into regions.","Convenience sampling uses whoever is easy to reach, with no random selection within groups.","Systematic sampling picks every nth record from one list rather than sampling within each region.",null],3],["dp38",0,2,"Before collecting product prices from a public website with a scraping script, what should the analyst check first?",["Whether the pages use HTTPS","The site's terms of use and whether it offers an API","How quickly the pages load on mobile and desktop browsers","Which web server software it runs"],1,"Terms of use (and robots.txt) may restrict automated collection, and an official API is usually more reliable than scraping. HTTPS and server software do not decide whether scraping is permitted.","Domain 2",["HTTPS protects data in transit but says nothing about whether automated collection is allowed.",null,"Page load speed affects how long scraping takes but not whether it is permitted or reliable.","The web server software does not determine whether scraping the site is allowed."],2],["dp39",0,3,"Most employees at a firm earn between 40,000 and 70,000, but three executives earn over 1 million. Which measure best describes a typical salary?",["Median","Mean","Range","Maximum"],0,"The median is the middle value and is not pulled by a few extreme salaries. The mean would be inflated by the executives and overstate what a typical employee earns.","Domain 3",[null,"The mean is pulled far upward by three salaries over 1 million, so it overstates a typical salary.","The range only describes the spread from lowest to highest pay, not a typical value.","The maximum is one executive's salary, the least typical value in the data."],1],["dp40",0,3,"What is the mean of the values 3, 5, 5, 8 and 9?",["5","8","30","6"],3,"The sum is 30 and there are 5 values, so the mean is 30 ÷ 5 = 6. Five is both the median and the mode, which is the common mix-up.","Domain 3",["5 is the median and the mode of this data, not the mean.","8 is just the fourth value in the list; the mean must account for all five values.","30 is the sum of the values; it still needs dividing by the count of 5.",null],1],["dp41",0,3,"A histogram of delivery times has a long tail to the right. How do the mean and median typically compare?",["The mean is less than the median","The mean and median are equal","The mode is greater than the mean","The mean is greater than the median"],3,"In a right-skewed distribution the long tail of high values pulls the mean above the median. They are equal only in a symmetric distribution.","Domain 3",["A mean below the median is typical of left skew, where the long tail is on the low side.","The mean and median are about equal only in a symmetric distribution, not one with a long right tail.","In right skew the mode is usually the smallest of the three measures, not larger than the mean.",null],2],["dp42",0,3,"Two call centers both average 5-minute calls. Center A's standard deviation is 0.5 minutes and Center B's is 3 minutes. What can you conclude?",["Center B's call times are much more variable","Center A handles more calls per day","Center B's true average must be higher","Center A's data must be skewed"],0,"Standard deviation measures spread around the mean, so B's call lengths vary far more. It says nothing about call volume, and both means are the same.","Domain 3",[null,"Standard deviation measures spread, not volume; it says nothing about how many calls each center handles.","Both centers have the same stated average of 5 minutes; a larger spread does not imply a higher mean.","A larger or smaller standard deviation alone does not show whether the data is skewed."],2],["dp43",0,3,"For a dataset, Q1 is 20 and Q3 is 50. Using the 1.5 × IQR rule, above what value is a point considered an outlier?",["75","80","125","95"],3,"IQR = 50 − 20 = 30, and 1.5 × 30 = 45. The upper fence is Q3 + 45 = 95. Adding 45 to the median or using Q1 gives the wrong fence.","Domain 3",["75 does not come from the rule; the upper fence is Q3 plus 1.5 times the IQR, which is 50 + 45 = 95.","80 is Q3 plus the IQR (50 + 30), leaving out the 1.5 multiplier.","125 is Q3 plus 2.5 times the IQR (50 + 75); the rule uses a multiplier of 1.5, giving 95.",null],3],["dp44",0,3,"For data that is approximately normally distributed, roughly what percentage of values fall within one standard deviation of the mean?",["68%","95%","50%","99.7%"],0,"The empirical rule says about 68% fall within 1 SD, 95% within 2 SD and 99.7% within 3 SD. 95% is the two-standard-deviation figure.","Domain 3",[null,"95% is the share within two standard deviations, not one.","50% is the share of values above (or below) the mean, not the share within one standard deviation.","99.7% is the share within three standard deviations of the mean."],1],["dp45",0,3,"Exam scores have a mean of 70 and a standard deviation of 5. What is the z-score of a student who scored 80?",["2","0.5","10","14"],0,"z = (80 − 70) ÷ 5 = 2, meaning the score is two standard deviations above the mean. 10 is only the raw difference, not divided by the standard deviation.","Domain 3",[null,"0.5 comes from dividing the standard deviation by the difference (5 ÷ 10), which inverts the formula.","10 is the raw difference from the mean; it has not been divided by the standard deviation.","14 is the mean divided by the standard deviation (70 ÷ 5), which is not a z-score."],2],["dp46",0,3,"Sales were 80,000 last quarter and 92,000 this quarter. What is the percent change?",["13%","12%","87%","15%"],3,"Percent change = (92,000 − 80,000) ÷ 80,000 = 0.15, or 15%. Dividing by the new value (92,000) gives about 13%, a common error.","Domain 3",["13% divides the 12,000 increase by the new value of 92,000 instead of the old value of 80,000.","12% confuses the 12,000 increase with a percentage without dividing by the base.","87% is 80,000 as a share of 92,000, a ratio of the two values rather than the change.",null],1],["dp47",0,3,"An A/B test of a new checkout page gives a p-value of 0.03, and the team set a significance level of 0.05. What is the correct conclusion?",["Fail to reject the null hypothesis","Reject the null hypothesis at the 5% level","The new page raised conversion by 3%","The test proves the null hypothesis is true"],1,"Because 0.03 is below 0.05, the result is statistically significant and the null hypothesis is rejected. The p-value is not the size of the effect, and tests never prove the null true.","Domain 3",["Failing to reject is correct only when the p-value is at or above alpha; 0.03 is below 0.05.",null,"A p-value is not the size of the effect, so it cannot show a 3% increase in conversion.","Hypothesis tests never prove the null true, and this result is evidence against it."],2],["dp48",0,3,"A study concludes that a new training program improves sales, but in reality it has no effect. What kind of error occurred?",["Type II error (false negative)","Sampling error","Type I error (false positive)","Measurement bias"],2,"Rejecting a null hypothesis that is actually true is a Type I error, a false positive. A Type II error is the opposite: missing a real effect.","Domain 3",["A Type II error misses a real effect; here the study found an effect that does not exist.","Sampling error is the natural random difference between a sample and the population, not the name of this wrong conclusion.",null,"Measurement bias comes from a flawed way of measuring; the scenario describes a false positive conclusion."],3],["dp49",0,3,"Holding everything else equal, what happens to the width of a 95% confidence interval when the sample size increases?",["It gets narrower","It gets wider","It does not change","It becomes a 99% interval"],0,"A larger sample reduces the standard error, so the interval narrows and the estimate is more precise. The confidence level stays at 95% unless you change it.","Domain 3",[null,"Larger samples reduce the standard error, so the interval shrinks rather than widens.","The width depends on the standard error, which falls as the sample grows, so it does change.","The confidence level is chosen by the analyst and stays at 95% regardless of sample size."],3],["dp50",0,3,"A city's data shows ice cream sales and drowning incidents are strongly positively correlated. What is the best interpretation?",["Eating ice cream raises drowning risk","Drowning incidents increase ice cream sales","A lurking variable such as hot weather may drive both","The correlation must come from a data-entry error in the city records"],2,"Correlation does not show causation; hot weather plausibly increases both swimming and ice cream sales. Both causal readings are unsupported by correlation alone.","Domain 3",["Correlation alone cannot show that ice cream causes drowning; a shared cause is far more plausible.","This reverses the direction of causation, and correlation does not support any causal direction by itself.",null,"A strong correlation with an obvious shared cause is not evidence of a data-entry error."],1],["dp51",0,3,"The correlation coefficient between product price and units sold is −0.85. How should this be read?",["A weak positive relationship","No linear relationship","Price increases cause an 85% drop in sales","A strong negative linear relationship"],3,"r ranges from −1 to 1; −0.85 means a strong negative linear association: as price rises, units tend to fall. r is not a percentage effect and does not prove causation.","Domain 3",["The negative sign means a negative relationship, and a magnitude of 0.85 is strong, not weak.","A value of -0.85 is far from zero, which indicates a strong linear relationship.","r is not a percentage effect and correlation alone does not show that price changes cause sales changes.",null],2],["dp52",0,3,"A regression model is sales = 200 + 15 × ad_spend, where ad_spend is in thousands of dollars. What sales does it predict when ad_spend is 10?",["215","350","3,000","2,150"],1,"Plug in 10: 200 + 15 × 10 = 350. 215 comes from adding 15 once instead of multiplying by 10.","Domain 3",["215 adds the slope once (200 + 15) instead of multiplying it by the ad spend of 10.",null,"3,000 multiplies the intercept by 15 instead of adding it to the slope times ad spend.","2,150 multiplies (200 + 15) by 10, applying the ad spend to the intercept as well."],2],["dp53",0,3,"A manager asks, 'Why did product returns spike in March?' Which type of analysis answers this?",["Predictive","Prescriptive","Diagnostic","Descriptive"],2,"Diagnostic analysis explains why something happened, for example by drilling into returns by product and reason. Descriptive analysis only reports that returns rose.","Domain 3",["Predictive analysis forecasts future outcomes; the question asks about a past spike.","Prescriptive analysis recommends an action; the manager first wants the cause explained.",null,"Descriptive analysis would report that returns rose, but not explain why they rose."],1],["dp54",0,3,"A warehouse wants an analysis that recommends the reorder quantity for each item that minimizes cost while avoiding stockouts. Which analysis type is this?",["Descriptive","Diagnostic","Predictive","Prescriptive"],3,"Prescriptive analysis recommends an action, often using optimization or simulation. Predictive analysis forecasts demand but does not by itself choose the best action.","Domain 3",["Descriptive analysis summarizes what already happened; it does not recommend a reorder quantity.","Diagnostic analysis explains why something happened, not what action to take next.","Predictive analysis can forecast demand, but on its own it does not choose the cost-minimizing action.",null],2],["dp55",0,3,"Given a new dataset, an analyst plots distributions, checks summary statistics and looks for patterns and anomalies before forming hypotheses. What is this called?",["Exploratory data analysis","Prescriptive analysis","Hypothesis testing","Data masking"],0,"Exploratory data analysis (EDA) is the open-ended first look at data to understand it and spot issues. Hypothesis testing comes later and checks a specific claim.","Domain 3",[null,"Prescriptive analysis recommends actions and comes much later than a first look at new data.","Hypothesis testing checks a specific claim; the analyst has not formed any hypothesis yet.","Data masking hides sensitive values; it is a protection technique, not an analysis step."],1],["dp56",0,3,"Which of these is the best-written KPI for a customer support team?",["Make customers feel much happier with support","Resolve 90% of tickets within 24 hours","Handle as many tickets as the team possibly can","Become the best-rated support team in the industry"],1,"A good KPI is specific, measurable and has a target and a time frame. The other options are goals without a measurable definition or target.","Domain 3",["Happiness is not defined or measured here, and there is no target or time frame.",null,"As many as possible sets no measurable target, so progress cannot be judged.","Best-rated is an aspiration without a defined measure, target or time frame."],2],["dp57",0,3,"A department budgeted 50,000 for a quarter and actually spent 56,000. What is the variance to plan?",["6,000 over budget (12%)","6,000 under budget (10.7%)","56,000 over budget","6,000 over budget (10.7%)"],0,"Variance = 56,000 − 50,000 = 6,000 over budget, and 6,000 ÷ 50,000 = 12%. Dividing by the actual (56,000) gives 10.7%, which uses the wrong base.","Domain 3",[null,"Spending 56,000 against a 50,000 budget is over budget, and 10.7% uses the actual as the base.","56,000 is the actual spend itself; the variance is the difference from the 50,000 budget.","The direction is right, but 10.7% divides by the actual; variance percent uses the budget as the base."],1],["dp58",0,3,"An analyst needs a running total of daily sales in date order, with one row per day kept in the output. Which SQL expression fits?",["SUM(sales) with GROUP BY sale_date","COUNT(*) OVER ()","SUM(sales) OVER (ORDER BY sale_date)","MAX(sales) with HAVING sale_date"],2,"A window function with ORDER BY accumulates the sum row by row while keeping each row. GROUP BY gives each day's own total, not a cumulative one.","Domain 3",["GROUP BY gives each day's own total, not a cumulative sum that builds from one day to the next.","COUNT(*) OVER () returns the total row count on every row, not a running sum of sales.",null,"MAX with HAVING filters groups by a condition; it does not accumulate sales over dates."],3],["dp59",0,3,"In a spreadsheet, an analyst must pull each product's price from a separate price table using the product ID. Which function fits?",["CONCAT","COUNTIF","ROUND","XLOOKUP"],3,"XLOOKUP (or VLOOKUP/INDEX-MATCH) finds a key in one range and returns a related value. COUNTIF only counts matching cells.","Domain 3",["CONCAT joins text values together; it cannot find a price by matching a product ID.","COUNTIF counts cells that meet a condition; it does not return a related value from another table.","ROUND changes the number of decimal places of a value; it does not look anything up.",null],2],["dp60",0,3,"A new dashboard shows monthly revenue exactly double the figure in the finance system. What is the most likely cause?",["The currency symbol is formatted incorrectly","A join duplicated rows, so amounts were summed twice","The chart uses a logarithmic axis","The report refresh ran too recently"],1,"Duplicated rows from a one-to-many join (fan-out) double the sums, which reconciliation against a trusted source reveals. Formatting and axis choices change display, not totals.","Domain 3",["A formatting problem changes how numbers look, not their size, so it cannot double the total.",null,"Axis scaling changes how the chart is drawn; it does not change the revenue figure shown.","A recent refresh would make data more current, not exactly double a monthly total."],3],["dp61",0,4,"A sales director wants to see how monthly revenue has changed over the last three years. Which chart is the best choice?",["Line chart","Pie chart","Scatter plot","Treemap"],0,"Line charts show trends over a continuous time axis clearly. A pie chart shows parts of a whole at one point in time and cannot show a trend.","Domain 4",[null,"A pie chart shows parts of a whole at one point in time and cannot show change across 36 months.","A scatter plot shows the relationship between two numeric variables, not a single measure's trend over time.","A treemap shows hierarchical parts of a whole by area, not how a value changes over time."],1],["dp62",0,4,"A slide must show how one year's budget is split across four departments, with the parts adding up to 100%. Which chart is simplest for this audience?",["Line chart","Histogram","Pie chart","Scatter plot"],2,"With only a few categories that sum to a whole, a pie (or a 100% stacked bar) shows part-to-whole at a glance. A histogram shows the distribution of one numeric variable.","Domain 4",["A line chart shows change over time; this is a single year's split across departments.","A histogram shows the distribution of one numeric variable in bins, not shares of a total.",null,"A scatter plot relates two numeric variables and cannot show four parts adding to 100%."],1],["dp63",0,4,"An analyst wants to show whether advertising spend and sales are related across 200 stores. Which chart fits?",["Scatter plot","Pie chart","Stacked area chart","Gauge"],0,"A scatter plot places each store at its spend and sales values, which reveals correlation and outliers. A stacked area chart shows composition over time.","Domain 4",[null,"A pie chart shows shares of one total; it cannot show how two numeric variables relate across stores.","A stacked area chart shows composition over time, not the relationship between spend and sales.","A gauge displays a single value against a target, not 200 paired data points."],1],["dp64",0,4,"Operations wants to see how delivery times are distributed: how many orders took 0–1 day, 1–2 days and so on. Which chart fits?",["Pie chart","Line chart","Filled map","Histogram"],3,"A histogram shows the frequency of values in numeric ranges (bins), which is exactly a distribution. A line chart is for trends over time.","Domain 4",["A pie chart shows categorical shares and loses the ordered numeric ranges that describe a distribution.","A line chart shows trends over time, not how often values fall in each delivery-time range.","A filled map shades geographic areas; delivery-time distribution has no geographic dimension here.",null],2],["dp65",0,4,"HR wants one visual comparing the median, spread and outliers of salaries across five departments. Which chart fits?",["Donut chart","Gauge","Box and whisker plot","Word cloud"],2,"A box plot shows the median, quartiles and outliers for each group side by side. A donut chart only shows parts of a whole.","Domain 4",["A donut chart shows parts of a whole, not medians, spread or outliers within groups.","A gauge shows one value against a target and cannot compare five distributions.",null,"A word cloud sizes words by frequency and has nothing to show about salary distributions."],2],["dp66",0,4,"Leadership wants to see total revenue for each US state and spot regional patterns at a glance. Which visual fits best?",["Line chart","Filled (choropleth) map","Gauge","Histogram"],1,"A filled map shades each geographic area by value, which makes regional patterns visible. A histogram groups numeric values and loses the geography.","Domain 4",["A line chart shows trends over time and hides the geographic pattern leadership wants.",null,"A gauge shows a single value and cannot display revenue for every state.","A histogram groups revenue values into bins and loses which state each value belongs to."],1],["dp67",0,4,"A bar chart comparing two satisfaction scores of 97 and 99 starts its y-axis at 95, making one bar look three times taller. What is the problem?",["It is missing a legend","It has too many categories","Its colors are not accessible","The truncated axis exaggerates the difference"],3,"Bar lengths should start at zero; a truncated axis makes a small difference look large. Adding a legend would not fix the distorted proportions.","Domain 4",["A legend identifies series; adding one would not fix bars that exaggerate a 2-point difference.","Two bars are not too many categories; the distortion comes from the axis.","Accessible colors matter, but they do not correct the misleading bar lengths.",null],2],["dp68",0,4,"A dashboard marks good results in green and bad results in red, with no other cue. Some users cannot tell them apart. What is the best improvement?",["Make the red and green shades brighter and more saturated","Add text labels or icons and a color-blind-safe palette","Switch to 3D charts","Remove the legend to reduce clutter"],1,"Red-green color vision deficiency is common, so meaning should never rely on color alone; add labels or icons and use a safe palette. Brighter red and green are still hard to distinguish.","Domain 4",["Brighter red and green are still hard to tell apart for people with red-green color vision deficiency.",null,"3D effects distort values and do nothing to help readers who cannot see the color difference.","Removing the legend makes the color coding harder to interpret for every reader."],1],["dp69",0,4,"Executives want a single page of key KPIs refreshed daily, with the option to click a region to see its detail. What should the analyst deliver?",["A static monthly PDF","A raw CSV extract","An interactive dashboard with drill-down","A one-off ad hoc query"],2,"An interactive dashboard refreshes on a schedule and supports filtering and drill-down. A static PDF cannot drill down and would be out of date between releases.","Domain 4",["A static monthly PDF cannot be refreshed daily or support clicking into a region.","A raw CSV extract has no KPI layout or interactivity and is not suited to executives.",null,"An ad hoc query answers a one-time question and does not give a reusable daily view."],2],["dp70",0,4,"A manager asks once for a list of customers who bought a recalled product last week, for a single meeting. What type of report is this?",["Ad hoc report","Recurring scheduled report","Operational dashboard","Self-service semantic model"],0,"An ad hoc report answers a one-time question. A recurring report is produced on a schedule, which this request does not need.","Domain 4",[null,"A recurring report runs on a schedule, but this request is a one-time need for a single meeting.","An operational dashboard monitors ongoing activity and is overkill for a single list.","A self-service semantic model lets users build their own reports; it is not a one-off list."],1],["dp71",0,4,"An analyst has 20 minutes with the executive team to present a churn analysis. How should the presentation start?",["Show every chart from the analysis","Lead with the main finding and its recommendation","Start by walking through the SQL code used for each step","Put all the raw tables and query results in the main body"],1,"Executives need the conclusion and what to do first, with supporting evidence after. Code and full tables belong in an appendix or a technical session.","Domain 4",["Showing every chart buries the key message and wastes the executives' limited time.",null,"Walking through SQL suits a technical audience, not executives who need the conclusion.","Raw tables in the main body overwhelm executives; they belong in an appendix."],2],["dp72",0,4,"A weekly report shows data through last Friday, but readers on Wednesday assume it is current. Which report element prevents this confusion?",["The company logo","A larger title font","A visible data as-of or refresh date","An extra appendix describing every field"],2,"An as-of or last-refreshed date tells readers how current the data is. Branding and fonts do not communicate data currency.","Domain 4",["A logo identifies the organization but says nothing about how current the data is.","A larger title font improves visibility but does not tell readers the data's cut-off date.",null,"An appendix of field definitions explains meanings but does not show when data was last refreshed."],2],["dp73",0,4,"In a formal report for executives, where should the detailed methodology, full data tables and field definitions go?",["The executive summary","The title","The chart legends","An appendix"],3,"An appendix keeps supporting detail available without cluttering the main story. The executive summary should stay short and focused on findings.","Domain 4",["The executive summary should stay short and focused on findings, not hold full tables.","The title names the report; it cannot hold methodology or data tables.","Chart legends identify series in a chart and are no place for full methodology or definitions.",null],2],["dp74",0,4,"Warehouse staff need a wall display of inventory levels that changes throughout the day as stock moves. Which refresh approach fits?",["A monthly snapshot","Real-time or frequent automated refresh","A quarterly printed report sent to each warehouse","A manual yearly import"],1,"Operational monitoring needs live or near-real-time data. Snapshots are fine for period-end reporting but would be stale within minutes here.","Domain 4",["A monthly snapshot would be stale within minutes on a busy warehouse floor.",null,"A quarterly printed report is months out of date for staff who need current stock levels.","A manual yearly import is far too infrequent for inventory that changes through the day."],1],["dp75",0,4,"On a sales dashboard, the Region filter lists the right values, but two of the charts do not change when a region is selected. What is the most likely cause?",["The chart colors are not accessible","The report title is too long","The data is stored as Parquet","The filter is not linked to those visuals"],3,"Filters apply only to visuals they are connected to (or through model relationships); a missing link or relationship leaves those charts unfiltered. Colors, titles and storage format do not affect filtering.","Domain 4",["Inaccessible colors affect readability, not whether a slicer filters a visual.","Title length has no effect on how filters interact with visuals.","The file format behind the data does not stop filters from applying to connected visuals.",null],3],["dp76",0,4,"A company style guide sets the fonts, color palette and logo placement for every report. What is its main purpose?",["It makes queries run faster","It satisfies HIPAA encryption rules","It keeps reports consistent and recognizable","It reduces storage costs"],2,"A style guide gives reports a consistent, trusted look and makes them easier to read. It has no effect on query speed, compliance encryption or storage.","Domain 4",["Fonts, colors and logos affect appearance, not how quickly queries run.","HIPAA safeguards concern protecting health information, not report fonts and logo placement.",null,"Visual styling rules do not change how much storage the data uses."],1],["dp77",0,4,"Two copies of the quarterly report are circulating with different numbers, and nobody knows which is current. Which practice prevents this?",["Using version numbers, dates and a change log","Emailing the report more often","Adding more colors to the charts","Removing dates from the file names to avoid confusion"],0,"Version control with clear numbering, dates and a change log shows which copy is current and what changed. Sending the report more often creates even more copies.","Domain 4",[null,"Sending the report more often creates even more copies without showing which one is current.","More chart colors change appearance but do nothing to identify the current version.","Removing dates makes it even harder to tell which copy is newest."],2],["dp78",0,4,"A dashboard of monthly summaries loads slowly because every visual queries a 500-million-row transaction table directly. What is the best fix?",["Add more visuals to each page","Replace every bar chart with a pie chart to simplify rendering","Remove the date filter","Point the visuals at a pre-aggregated summary table"],3,"Pre-aggregating to the level the visuals need (for example monthly by region) means far fewer rows to scan. Removing the date filter would make queries read even more data.","Domain 4",["More visuals add more queries against the huge table and make loading slower.","Pie charts query the same data, and swapping chart types does not reduce rows scanned.","Removing the date filter makes each query scan even more of the 500 million rows.",null],3],["dp79",0,5,"The VP of HR decides who may access employee compensation data and approves its classification level. Which governance role is the VP playing?",["Data custodian","Data owner","Data consumer","Data steward"],1,"The data owner is the accountable business leader who decides classification and access. The custodian implements those decisions technically, and the steward looks after definitions and quality.","Domain 5",["The custodian implements technical controls such as permissions and backups but does not decide who gets access.",null,"A data consumer uses data under policy and does not approve access or classification.","A steward maintains definitions and quality but is not the accountable decision-maker for access."],2],["dp80",0,5,"A database administrator configures backups, permissions and encryption on the HR database as the business directs. Which role is this?",["Data owner","Data consumer","Data custodian","Data subject"],2,"The custodian operates and protects the data technically on the owner's behalf. The owner makes the decisions but usually does not implement the controls.","Domain 5",["The owner decides classification and access but does not usually configure the database.","A consumer uses the data for work and does not run backups or set encryption.",null,"The data subject is the person the data describes, not someone who administers it."],2],["dp81",0,5,"Sales and Finance define 'active customer' differently, so their reports disagree. What best fixes this?",["A shared data dictionary with one agreed definition","A new firewall rule","A larger database server","A more frequent backup schedule for both departments"],0,"A data dictionary or business glossary records one approved definition for each metric and field. Infrastructure changes do not resolve conflicting business definitions.","Domain 5",[null,"A firewall rule controls network traffic and cannot reconcile two business definitions.","More server capacity changes performance, not how 'active customer' is defined.","Backups protect against data loss but do nothing about conflicting definitions."],1],["dp82",0,5,"Auditors ask where a KPI in the board report came from and which transformations were applied to it along the way. What documentation answers this?",["Data masking","Data sovereignty","A retention schedule","Data lineage"],3,"Data lineage traces data from its sources through each transformation to the report. A retention schedule only says how long data is kept.","Domain 5",["Masking hides sensitive values; it does not record where a figure came from or how it was transformed.","Data sovereignty concerns which country's laws apply to data, not the path a KPI took.","A retention schedule says how long records are kept, not how a number was produced.",null],3],["dp83",0,5,"Profiling shows that 15% of customer records have a blank email address, which is a required field. Which data quality dimension is affected?",["Timeliness","Completeness","Uniqueness","Validity"],1,"Completeness measures whether required values are present. Validity would apply if emails were present but badly formatted.","Domain 5",["Timeliness is about how current data is; these emails are absent, not out of date.",null,"Uniqueness concerns duplicate records, and blank fields are not duplicates.","Validity would apply if emails were present but badly formatted; here they are blank."],1],["dp84",0,5,"A date-of-birth field contains the value 2025-02-30. Which data quality dimension does this record fail?",["Completeness","Timeliness","Validity","Uniqueness"],2,"February 30 is not a real date, so the value breaks the allowed format or range and fails validity. The field is filled in, so completeness is not the issue.","Domain 5",["The field is filled in, so completeness is not the problem.","Timeliness concerns how current data is, not whether a date can exist.",null,"Uniqueness concerns duplicate records, not an impossible calendar date."],2],["dp85",0,5,"A customer's address differs between the CRM, billing and shipping systems. Which practice establishes one authoritative, shared version of each customer record?",["Data masking","Master data management","Data archiving","Tokenization"],1,"Master data management consolidates and governs core entities such as customers and products into a single source of truth. Masking and tokenization protect data but do not reconcile it.","Domain 5",["Masking hides sensitive values but does not reconcile conflicting addresses into one record.",null,"Archiving moves inactive data to long-term storage and does not resolve inconsistencies.","Tokenization replaces sensitive values with tokens but does not create one authoritative customer record."],2],["dp86",0,5,"Developers need realistic customer data in a test environment, but they must not see real names or card numbers. What should the team do?",["Copy production data unchanged","Disable access logging","Export the data to a spreadsheet","Mask the sensitive fields"],3,"Data masking replaces sensitive values with realistic but fictitious ones, so tests still work without exposing real data. Copying production data unchanged exposes it to more people.","Domain 5",["Copying production data unchanged exposes real names and card numbers to developers.","Disabling access logging removes oversight and does nothing to hide sensitive values.","Exporting to a spreadsheet creates another uncontrolled copy of the same real data.",null],2],["dp87",0,5,"Which of these is protected health information (PHI)?",["A company's quarterly revenue total","A published product catalog","A patient's diagnosis linked to their name","Weather readings for a city"],2,"PHI is health information that can be tied to an individual, such as a diagnosis with a name. The other options contain no personal health data.","Domain 5",["Company revenue is business data about the organization, not health information about a person.","A published catalog is public product data with no personal health information.",null,"City weather readings contain no information about any individual's health."],1],["dp88",0,5,"A company collects personal data from people in the European Union and must honor requests to erase their data and limit transfers outside the EU. Which regulation applies?",["GDPR","HIPAA","PCI DSS","SOX"],0,"The GDPR governs personal data of people in the EU, including the right to erasure and rules on international transfers. HIPAA covers US health information.","Domain 5",[null,"HIPAA covers US health information held by covered entities, not EU personal data in general.","PCI DSS is an industry standard for payment card data, not a law granting erasure rights.","SOX governs financial reporting controls for US public companies, not personal data rights."],1],["dp89",0,5,"Before sharing a research dataset, a team replaces names with random IDs but keeps a secured key table that can link the IDs back to people. What technique is this?",["Anonymization","Pseudonymization","Encryption at rest","Aggregation"],1,"Pseudonymization replaces identifiers but remains reversible with the key, so the data is still personal data. True anonymization would make re-identification impossible.","Domain 5",["Anonymization is irreversible, but here a key table can still link IDs back to people.",null,"Encryption at rest protects stored data with keys; it does not describe swapping names for random IDs.","Aggregation summarizes many records into totals, but this dataset still has one row per person."],3],["dp90",0,5,"Policy requires transaction records to be kept for seven years and then destroyed. An audit finds eight-year-old records still on a former analyst's laptop. Which life-cycle stage failed?",["Collection","Classification","Sharing","Secure disposal"],3,"Records past their retention period should have been securely destroyed everywhere, including local copies. Collection and classification happened correctly at the start of the life cycle.","Domain 5",["Collection is where data enters the organization; the failure happened at the end of the life cycle.","Classification assigns a sensitivity level and would not remove records once their retention period ended.","Sharing covers distributing data to others; the problem is that expired copies were never destroyed.",null],1],["x1",0,1,"A product dimension stores each product's category. When a category name is corrected from 'Hardwear' to 'Hardware', the business wants the fix applied everywhere with no history kept. Which slowly changing dimension approach fits?",["Type 1: overwrite the attribute in place","Type 2: add a new row with effective dates","Type 3: add a column holding the previous value","Type 0: keep the original value and never change it"],0,"Type 1 simply overwrites the old value, which suits corrections where history has no business meaning. Type 2 is tempting because it is the most common SCD type, but it would keep the misspelled category as a historical version.","Domain 1: Slowly changing dimensions",[null,"Type 2 preserves full history by adding rows, which the business explicitly does not want for a typo fix.","Type 3 keeps the prior value in an extra column, so the misspelled name would still be stored.","Type 0 never updates the attribute, so the misspelling would remain in every report."],2],["x2",0,1,"An online store sells laptops, shirts and books. Each product type has very different attributes, and new attributes are added often. Which database type handles this MOST naturally?",["A document database with flexible JSON-like documents","A relational table with one column for every possible attribute","A key-value store that saves the whole catalog under a single key","A graph database that links each attribute to products as nodes"],0,"Document databases let each record carry its own set of fields, so varied and changing product attributes fit without schema changes. A wide relational table is the tempting alternative, but it creates sparse columns and frequent schema migrations.","Domain 1: Non-relational databases",[null,"A single wide relational table would be mostly empty columns and would need schema changes for every new attribute.","One key for the whole catalog makes it impossible to query or update individual products efficiently.","Graph databases are built for traversing relationships, not for storing variable attribute sets per item."],2],["x3",0,1,"A modeler is designing a sales fact table. Analysts need to report sales by individual receipt line, by product and by hour. What should the modeler define FIRST?",["The grain: what one row of the fact table represents","The color scheme that dashboards built on the table will use","The list of aggregate tables that will summarize it later","The indexes that the reporting queries will need most"],0,"Declaring the grain comes first in dimensional design because it determines which dimensions and measures belong in the table; here the grain is one receipt line. Aggregates are tempting to plan early, but they depend on the grain being settled.","Domain 1: Dimensional modeling",[null,"Dashboard colors are a presentation concern and do not affect how the fact table is structured.","Aggregate tables are derived from the fact table, so they can only be designed once its grain is known.","Indexes are tuned for known queries after the table structure, including its grain, has been decided."],3],["x4",0,1,"An Orders table repeats the customer's full mailing address on every order row. When a customer moves, some rows get updated and others do not. What design change BEST prevents this?",["Add a trigger that copies the new address into every matching order row","Normalize the design so the address is stored once in a Customers table","Keep the design but add a column recording when each address was last edited","Denormalize further by also copying the address into the Payments table"],1,"Storing the address once and referencing it by key removes the redundancy that causes update anomalies, which is the purpose of normalization. A trigger can keep copies in sync, but it hides the root cause rather than fixing it.","Domain 1: Normalization",["A trigger treats the symptom and adds maintenance risk, while the redundant copies of the address remain.",null,"An edit timestamp shows when a row changed but does not stop rows from disagreeing with each other.","Copying the address into more tables adds redundancy and makes the update anomaly worse."],2],["x5",0,1,"After an import, a Quantity column sorts as 1, 10, 100, 2, 25, 3 instead of numerically. What is the MOST likely cause?",["The column contains duplicate values","The values were imported as text strings","The sort was applied in descending order","The column has too many null values"],1,"Text is sorted character by character, so '10' comes before '2'; converting the column to an integer type fixes it. Descending order is the tempting distractor, but it would still sort by numeric value.","Domain 1: Data types",["Duplicates would not change the order in which distinct values are sorted.",null,"Descending order would put the largest numbers first, not produce this character-by-character pattern.","Nulls sort together at one end and do not cause numbers to be ordered character by character."],1],["x6",0,1,"A global app records event timestamps from servers in New York, London and Tokyo, each using its local time. Analysts keep getting the order of events wrong. What is the BEST storage practice?",["Store timestamps as text so each region keeps its own format","Round every timestamp to the nearest day to hide the differences","Store in UTC and convert to local time only for display","Store only the date and drop the time portion entirely"],2,"Storing one standard, UTC, makes timestamps directly comparable, and local time can be applied when showing results. Storing text looks flexible but keeps the inconsistency and breaks date math.","Domain 1: Data types",["Text timestamps in mixed formats are harder to compare and still carry inconsistent time zones.","Rounding to days throws away the detail needed to order events within a day.",null,"Dropping the time makes it impossible to sequence events that happen on the same date."],2],["x7",0,1,"The marketing department wants a small, subject-focused store of campaign and lead data for its own reporting, fed from the enterprise data warehouse. What is this called?",["A data lake","An operational data store","A staging area","A data mart"],3,"A data mart is a subject-oriented subset of warehouse data built for one business area such as marketing. A data lake is tempting because it is also a repository, but it stores raw, broad data rather than a focused departmental model.","Domain 1: Data warehouses and marts",["A data lake holds raw data of many types for many users, not a curated departmental subset.","An operational data store holds current, integrated operational data, not a department-focused analytic subset.","A staging area is a temporary landing zone used during loading, not a reporting store for a department.",null],1],["x8",0,1,"A company wants to keep raw files cheaply in open formats on object storage, yet also run SQL with ACID transactions and schema enforcement for BI on the same data. Which architecture fits BEST?",["A lakehouse","A traditional OLTP database","A flat-file share on a network drive","A key-value cache"],0,"A lakehouse adds warehouse features such as ACID tables and schema enforcement on top of low-cost lake storage. An OLTP database gives transactions but not cheap open-format storage for raw analytic data.","Domain 1: Data lakes and lakehouses",[null,"An OLTP database is built for many small transactions, not for cheap storage of raw files in open formats.","A file share offers no SQL engine, transactions or schema enforcement for BI.","A cache holds short-lived values in memory for speed and is not a durable analytic store."],2],["x9",0,1,"A customer dimension is loaded from two source systems whose customer IDs overlap, and the warehouse also tracks history with new rows. What should serve as the dimension's primary key?",["The customer ID from whichever source system loaded first","The customer's email address","A system-generated surrogate key with no business meaning","A combination of customer name and date of birth"],2,"A surrogate key is unique per dimension row, independent of source systems, and supports multiple history rows per customer. Using a source customer ID is tempting, but overlapping IDs and history rows make it non-unique.","Domain 1: Keys and dimensional modeling",["Source IDs overlap across the two systems and repeat across history rows, so they cannot uniquely identify a row.","Email addresses change, can be shared and repeat across history rows, so they are poor primary keys.",null,"Names and birth dates can collide and would repeat across history rows for the same customer."],3],["x10",0,1,"An analyst wants to paste a spreadsheet of customer names, phone numbers and complaint notes into a free public AI chatbot to summarize common themes. What should the analyst do FIRST?",["Paste only half of the rows to limit the amount of data shared","Use an approved tool, or remove personal data first","Ask the chatbot to promise that it will not store the data","Convert the spreadsheet to PDF so the personal data is harder to read"],1,"Customer personal data should only go to AI tools the organization has approved, or be de-identified first, because public services may retain or reuse input. Sharing fewer rows feels safer but still discloses personal data.","Domain 1: AI tools and data handling",["Sharing half the rows still exposes real customers' personal data to an unapproved service.",null,"A chatbot's reply is not a binding data protection control and does not change how the service handles input.","A PDF contains the same personal data, so converting the format gives no protection."],2],["x11",0,1,"In a comma-separated file, addresses such as '12 Main St, Apt 4' split into two columns during import. What is the BEST fix?",["Delete all commas from the source system permanently","Import the file as a fixed-width file instead","Split the address column manually after each import","Wrap fields in quotation marks as text qualifiers"],3,"Text qualifiers tell the parser that commas inside quotes belong to the value, so the address stays in one column. Removing commas from the source is tempting but changes real data to work around a formatting issue.","Domain 1: File formats",["Changing source data to remove commas alters real values and is not a sound fix for a file format issue.","Fixed-width parsing needs every field padded to set positions, which this CSV does not have.","Manual splitting every time is error-prone and does not fix the underlying file format problem.",null],1],["x12",0,2,"An analyst receives a new customer extract and must clean it for reporting. What should the analyst do FIRST?",["Profile it: value ranges, nulls, types and distinct counts","Build the final dashboard to see which fields look wrong","Delete every row that contains any null value","Apply the same cleaning script used on a different dataset last month"],0,"Profiling reveals what problems actually exist, so cleaning steps can be chosen deliberately. Reusing last month's script seems efficient, but it assumes this extract has the same issues.","Domain 2: Data profiling",[null,"Building visuals before understanding the data can hide or misrepresent quality problems.","Dropping every row with a null may discard most of the data and introduce bias.","Another dataset's script may not match this file's structure or its specific problems."],1],["x13",0,2,"A merged contact list contains the same person several times with identical email, name and phone values. What cleaning step addresses this?",["Normalization to a 0 to 1 scale","Imputation of missing values","Deduplication based on the matching fields","Binning the records into groups"],2,"Deduplication keeps one record per real-world entity using key fields such as email. Imputation is a common cleaning step, but it addresses missing values, not repeated rows.","Domain 2: Data cleansing",["Scaling changes numeric ranges and does nothing about repeated records.","Imputation fills in missing values; these rows are complete but repeated.",null,"Binning groups continuous values into ranges and does not remove repeated rows."],1],["x14",0,2,"A query uses Customers LEFT JOIN Orders ON CustomerID and then adds WHERE Orders.OrderDate >= '2025-01-01'. Customers with no orders have disappeared from the results. Why?",["LEFT JOIN never returns customers who have no matching orders","The WHERE filter rejects the NULL order dates, which turns it into an inner join","OrderDate must be converted to text first, because SQL cannot compare date values directly","The join must be written as a RIGHT JOIN to keep all customers"],1,"Unmatched customers get NULL in OrderDate, and a WHERE comparison against NULL is not true, so those rows are removed; moving the date condition into the ON clause keeps them. Believing LEFT JOIN drops unmatched rows is the tempting misread.","Domain 2: SQL joins",["A LEFT JOIN does return unmatched customers with NULLs; the later WHERE filter is what removes them.",null,"Converting dates to text would make the comparison less reliable and does not explain the missing rows.","A RIGHT JOIN would keep all orders instead, which is the opposite of what is needed."],3],["x15",0,2,"After a nightly load from the order system into the warehouse, how can the team BEST verify that the load was complete and accurate?",["Check that the load job's log finished with no errors or warning messages of any kind","Open the target table and look at the first 20 rows","Ask report users whether numbers look normal the next morning","Reconcile row counts and control totals between source and target"],3,"Comparing record counts and control totals between source and target is a direct, repeatable test of completeness and accuracy. A clean job log is tempting evidence, but jobs can succeed while silently skipping or altering rows.","Domain 2: Data validation",["A clean log shows the job ran but does not prove every row arrived with the right values.","Spot-checking a few rows cannot detect missing rows or wrong totals across the whole table.","User impressions are slow and subjective, and small gaps can go unnoticed for a long time.",null],2],["x16",0,2,"A merged file has dates such as 03/04/2025 coming from both US and European offices. What should the analyst do BEFORE converting the column to a date type?",["Convert everything using the US month/day format, since US records appear first in the file","Identify each record's source format, then parse it explicitly to ISO dates","Let the spreadsheet guess the format automatically on import","Remove every date whose day value is 12 or lower"],1,"Values such as 03/04 are ambiguous, so the source format must be known per record and parsed explicitly into an unambiguous ISO date. Letting the tool guess is tempting but quietly swaps day and month for some rows.","Domain 2: Data transformation",["Assuming one format silently swaps day and month for records from the other offices.",null,"Automatic guessing uses one locale and will misread ambiguous values without any warning.","Deleting ambiguous dates throws away valid data instead of interpreting it correctly."],3],["x17",0,2,"A REST API returns 100 records per call and includes a 'next' token in each response. The analyst's script only ever receives 100 records. What is the MOST likely fix?",["Switch the request from HTTPS to plain HTTP so the server can send larger responses","Ask the provider to email a full CSV export every day","Loop through the pages, passing each 'next' token until none is returned","Increase the script's timeout value to one hour"],2,"The API is paginated, so the script must request each subsequent page using the token until the data is exhausted. A longer timeout seems plausible, but the call is completing normally with one page.","Domain 2: APIs and data acquisition",["Dropping encryption does not change page size and exposes the data and credentials in transit.","A manual export avoids the API rather than fixing the script, and it adds delay and manual work.",null,"The call is not timing out; it returns one page as designed, so a longer timeout changes nothing."],2],["x18",0,2,"To estimate satisfaction among all customers, a team surveys only the people who visited the head office lobby this week. What is the main weakness of this sample?",["A convenience sample, likely unrepresentative of all customers","It is a stratified sample, which always overstates satisfaction","The sample is too random to produce stable results","It is a census, so the results cannot be generalized"],0,"Choosing whoever is easy to reach produces a convenience sample, which can over-represent certain customers and bias the results. Calling it stratified is tempting, but no groups were defined and sampled deliberately.","Domain 2: Sampling",[null,"A stratified sample divides the population into groups and samples each; lobby visitors were not chosen that way.","Nothing about who happened to be in the lobby was random, so randomness is not the problem.","A census surveys the entire population; lobby visitors are a small, self-selected subset."],2],["x19",0,2,"An Orders table has one row per order, and many customers placed several orders. Which SQL expression returns how many different customers placed orders?",["COUNT(*)","SUM(CustomerID)","MAX(CustomerID)","COUNT(DISTINCT CustomerID)"],3,"COUNT(DISTINCT CustomerID) counts each customer once no matter how many orders they placed. COUNT(*) is the tempting choice, but it counts orders rather than customers.","Domain 2: SQL aggregation",["COUNT(*) counts every order row, so repeat customers are counted more than once.","Summing ID numbers produces a meaningless total, not a count of customers.","MAX returns the highest ID value, which is not the number of customers.",null],1],["x20",0,2,"A join on ProductCode matches fewer rows than expected. Profiling shows some codes are stored as 'AB12 ' with a trailing space in one table. What should the analyst apply?",["Trim leading and trailing whitespace from the codes before joining","Convert the codes to uppercase only, leaving other characters as they are","Round the codes to the nearest whole number","Change the join to a CROSS JOIN"],0,"Trimming removes the invisible spaces so identical codes compare as equal. Uppercasing is a common standardization step, but it does not touch whitespace.","Domain 2: Data cleansing",[null,"Changing case does not remove the extra space, so the values still will not match.","The codes are text identifiers, and rounding does not apply to them or remove spaces.","A cross join pairs every row with every other row, producing wrong results instead of correct matches."],1],["x21",0,2,"A customer table has a BirthDate column, and a report needs each customer's age as of the report date. What is the BEST approach?",["Ask customers to enter their current age in a new text field","Add a derived column computing age from BirthDate and the report date","Type each customer's age into a new column by hand","Replace BirthDate with the age calculated today and delete the original"],1,"A derived column computes age from the stored birth date, so it stays correct for any report date. Replacing BirthDate is tempting for simplicity, but it discards the data needed to recalculate age later.","Domain 2: Derived variables",["Self-reported ages go stale every year and a text field invites inconsistent entries.",null,"Manual entry is slow, error-prone and becomes wrong as time passes.","Overwriting BirthDate loses the source value, so ages cannot be recalculated for later reports."],2],["x22",0,2,"A store's point-of-sale system writes 0 in DailySales on days when the register was offline and no data was captured. Averages now look too low. What should the analyst do?",["Leave the zeros because zero is a valid number","Replace the zeros with the highest daily sales value in the dataset","Recode those zeros as missing (NULL) and document the change","Delete the entire DailySales column from the dataset"],2,"Zeros that represent missing capture should be treated as missing, not as real zero sales, so summary statistics are not distorted. Keeping them because zero is a valid number is tempting, but it misstates what actually happened.","Domain 2: Missing data",["These zeros mean 'no data', not 'no sales', so keeping them biases the average downward.","Filling gaps with the maximum inflates the average and invents data.",null,"Deleting the column removes all the valid sales data along with the bad values."],3],["x23",0,2,"A regression model needs to use a Region column with values North, South, East and West. Which transformation prepares it correctly?",["Assign North=1, South=2, East=3, West=4 and treat it as a continuous number","One-hot encode Region into indicator (dummy) columns","Sort the rows alphabetically by Region","Drop the Region column because models cannot use text"],1,"One-hot encoding creates a 0/1 column per category so the model does not assume a false order between regions. Simple numeric codes are tempting, but they impose a meaningless ranking and distance.","Domain 2: Data transformation",["Numbering categories implies an order and spacing, such as West being four times North, that does not exist.",null,"Sorting rows changes their order but gives the model no numeric representation of Region.","Categorical variables can be used once encoded, so dropping Region throws away useful information."],3],["x24",0,2,"An analyst needs a new column that shows 'High' when OrderTotal is over 500 and 'Standard' otherwise, inside a SQL SELECT statement. Which construct fits?",["A HAVING clause","A UNION operator","A CASE expression","A GROUP BY clause"],2,"A CASE expression evaluates conditions per row and returns a value, such as 'High' or 'Standard', for a new column. HAVING is tempting because it uses conditions too, but it filters grouped results.","Domain 2: SQL",["HAVING filters groups after aggregation and does not create a labeled column.","UNION stacks the results of two queries and does not label individual rows.",null,"GROUP BY collapses rows into groups rather than adding a label to each row."],1],["x25",0,3,"A shoe retailer wants to know which shoe size is sold most often so it can stock more of it. Which measure answers this?",["Mode","Mean","Range","Standard deviation"],0,"The mode is the most frequently occurring value, which is exactly the best-selling size. The mean is tempting as the usual 'average', but it may not match any real size.","Domain 3: Descriptive statistics",[null,"The mean size could be a size nobody buys, such as 8.37, and does not show the most frequent value.","The range shows the gap between the smallest and largest sizes, not which is most common.","Standard deviation measures spread and says nothing about which size is most popular."],1],["x26",0,3,"What is the median of 4, 7, 9 and 12?",["7","9","8","16"],2,"With an even count, the median is the average of the two middle values: (7 + 9) / 2 = 8. Picking 7 or 9 is tempting, but with four values there is no single middle value.","Domain 3: Descriptive statistics",["7 is the lower of the two middle values, not the median of an even-sized set.","9 is the upper of the two middle values, not the median of an even-sized set.",null,"16 is the sum of the two middle values; they must be averaged, not added."],1],["x27",0,3,"Store A had 100 orders averaging $20. Store B had 300 orders averaging $40. What is the average order value across both stores?",["$30","$35","$40","$60"],1,"Weight each average by its order count: (100 × 20 + 300 × 40) / 400 = 14,000 / 400 = $35. Averaging the two averages to get $30 is tempting but ignores the different volumes.","Domain 3: Weighted averages",["$30 is the simple average of the two store averages, which ignores that Store B had three times as many orders.",null,"$40 is only Store B's average and ignores Store A's orders entirely.","$60 adds the two averages together instead of weighting them by order count."],2],["x28",0,3,"An analyst wants to test whether preferred contact channel (email, phone, chat) is associated with customer region (four regions), using counts of customers. Which test fits BEST?",["A two-sample t-test","Simple linear regression","A paired t-test","A chi-square test of independence"],3,"The chi-square test of independence checks whether two categorical variables are related using a table of counts. A t-test is tempting as a familiar hypothesis test, but it needs a numeric variable.","Domain 3: Hypothesis testing",["A two-sample t-test compares the means of a numeric variable between two groups, but both variables here are categorical.","Linear regression models a numeric outcome from predictors; contact channel is a category, not a number.","A paired t-test compares two related numeric measurements, such as before and after, for the same subjects.",null],3],["x29",0,3,"A company wants to know whether average order value differs between customers who used a coupon and customers who did not. Which test is MOST appropriate?",["A chi-square goodness-of-fit test","A two-sample t-test","A correlation coefficient","A frequency table of coupon codes"],1,"A two-sample t-test compares the means of a numeric variable, order value, between two independent groups. Correlation is tempting, but it describes association between two numeric variables rather than comparing group means.","Domain 3: Hypothesis testing",["A goodness-of-fit test compares category counts to an expected distribution, not means between groups.",null,"Correlation measures the linear relationship between two numeric variables, not a difference in means between groups.","A frequency table only counts coupon codes and does not test for a difference in order value."],2],["x30",0,3,"Hospital A has a higher overall survival rate than Hospital B, yet Hospital B has a higher survival rate for both mild and severe cases when they are viewed separately. What explains this?",["A data entry error, since this result is mathematically impossible","The survival rates were calculated using medians instead of means","Simpson's paradox, from different mixes of case severity","A Type I error in the hypothesis test"],2,"Simpson's paradox occurs when a trend within subgroups reverses in the combined data because the groups have different sizes or mixes, such as B treating more severe cases. Assuming a data error is tempting, but the reversal is a genuine statistical effect.","Domain 3: Interpreting results",["This reversal is mathematically possible when group sizes differ, so it does not prove an error.","Survival rates are proportions, and switching between medians and means does not produce this reversal.",null,"A Type I error is a false positive in a test decision, not an explanation for rates reversing when data is grouped."],3],["x31",0,3,"A toy retailer's December sales are always far higher than November's. Leadership wants to judge whether this December was actually a good month. Which comparison is MOST meaningful?",["December versus November of this year","December versus the average of all months this year","December versus the lowest month this year","This December versus last December (year over year)"],3,"Year-over-year comparison matches the same season, removing the predictable holiday effect so real change is visible. Month-over-month is tempting because it is recent, but seasonality dominates it.","Domain 3: Trend analysis",["Month over month mixes in the normal holiday surge, so December will almost always look better than November.","Comparing to the yearly average still includes the seasonal spike and does not isolate true performance.","Comparing to the weakest month exaggerates the result and says nothing about real growth.",null],2],["x32",0,3,"Daily website visits jump up and down so much that the underlying trend is hard to see. Which technique helps reveal the trend?",["Calculate a 7-day moving average","Delete the days with the highest visits","Sort the days from highest to lowest","Show only the first day of each month"],0,"A moving average smooths short-term noise by averaging each day with its neighbors, making the direction of the trend clearer. Removing peaks also reduces spikes, but it throws away real data and biases the result.","Domain 3: Trend analysis",[null,"Removing high days distorts the data and hides real variation instead of smoothing it.","Sorting by value destroys the time order needed to see a trend.","Sampling one day per month discards most of the data and can be misleading."],1],["x33",0,3,"Delivery times have a variance of 16 when measured in minutes squared. What is the standard deviation, and why is it easier to report?",["16 minutes, because variance and standard deviation are always equal","8 minutes, because it is half the variance","4 minutes, because it is in the same units as the data","256 minutes, because it is the variance squared"],2,"Standard deviation is the square root of variance, √16 = 4, and it is expressed in minutes like the original data, so it is easier to interpret. Halving the variance is a tempting shortcut but is not the correct formula.","Domain 3: Measures of dispersion",["Variance and standard deviation are equal only when the value is 0 or 1, not in general.","Standard deviation is the square root of variance, not half of it.",null,"Squaring the variance moves further from the original units instead of back to them."],2],["x34",0,3,"A new fraud rule really does reduce fraud, but the pilot study concludes that it has no significant effect. What kind of error is this?",["Type I error (false positive)","Rounding error","Sampling frame error","Type II error (false negative)"],3,"Failing to detect an effect that really exists is a Type II error, often caused by too small a sample. Type I is the tempting confusion, but it describes falsely detecting an effect.","Domain 3: Hypothesis testing",["A Type I error means finding an effect that does not exist, the opposite of this situation.","Rounding affects numeric precision and is not a type of hypothesis-testing error.","A sampling frame error is about using the wrong list of the population, not about the test conclusion itself.",null],2],["x35",0,3,"Annual revenue grew from 100,000 to 121,000 over two years. What was the compound annual growth rate (CAGR)?",["21%","10.5%","11%","10%"],3,"CAGR = (121,000 / 100,000)^(1/2) − 1 = √1.21 − 1 = 0.10, or 10% per year. Dividing the 21% total by two is tempting but ignores compounding.","Domain 3: Growth calculations",["21% is the total growth over two years, not the annual compound rate.","10.5% is the total growth divided by two, which ignores compounding.","11% compounded for two years would give about 123,210, not 121,000.",null],3],["x36",0,3,"A planning team uses three years of sales history to estimate next quarter's demand for each product. Which type of analysis is this?",["Descriptive","Diagnostic","Predictive","Exploratory"],2,"Forecasting future demand from historical data is predictive analysis. Descriptive is tempting because the history is summarized along the way, but the goal here is a future estimate.","Domain 3: Types of analysis",["Descriptive analysis summarizes what already happened rather than estimating future values.","Diagnostic analysis explains why something happened in the past, not what will happen next.",null,"Exploratory analysis investigates data for patterns and questions; it does not produce a forecast by itself."],1],["x37",0,3,"Sales reps are ranked by revenue, and two reps tie for second place. The business wants the next rep after the tie to be ranked 4th. Which SQL window function produces this?",["ROW_NUMBER()","DENSE_RANK()","NTILE(4)","RANK()"],3,"RANK gives tied rows the same rank and then skips numbers, so after two reps at 2 the next is 4. DENSE_RANK is the tempting near-miss, but it would assign 3 with no gap.","Domain 3: SQL window functions",["ROW_NUMBER gives every row a unique number, so tied reps would get different ranks.","DENSE_RANK leaves no gaps, so the rep after the tie would be ranked 3rd.","NTILE divides rows into a fixed number of buckets and does not rank individual reps.",null],3],["x38",0,3,"A regression model predicting monthly sales from ad spend has an R-squared of 0.64. What does this mean?",["64% of the variation in sales is explained by the model","The model's predictions are correct 64% of the time","Every $1 of ad spend increases sales by $0.64","There is a 64% chance that the model is valid"],0,"R-squared is the proportion of variance in the outcome that the model explains, here 64%. Reading it as the slope is a common mistake; the slope coefficient describes the change per dollar.","Domain 3: Regression",[null,"R-squared is not an accuracy rate for individual predictions being right or wrong.","The effect of each dollar is given by the slope coefficient, not by R-squared.","R-squared is not a probability that the model is valid."],2],["x39",0,4,"A chart must compare revenue for 15 product categories whose names are long, such as 'Outdoor furniture and garden décor'. Which chart is BEST?",["A horizontal bar chart sorted by revenue","A pie chart with 15 slices","A line chart with categories on the x-axis","A scatter plot of category versus revenue"],0,"Horizontal bars leave room for long labels, and sorting makes ranking easy to read. A line chart is tempting for many points, but it suggests a trend between unrelated categories.","Domain 4: Chart selection",[null,"Fifteen pie slices are hard to compare and the long labels would crowd around the circle.","Lines imply a continuous sequence, such as time, which unordered categories do not have.","Scatter plots show relationships between two numeric variables, not comparisons across categories."],1],["x40",0,4,"Sales leadership wants each region's quarterly revenue shown against its target, in a compact form that fits many KPIs on one dashboard. Which visual is BEST?",["A 3D pie chart for each region","A word cloud of region names","A bullet chart showing actual versus target","A table listing every individual transaction"],2,"A bullet chart shows the actual value, the target marker and qualitative ranges in a small, linear space. A 3D pie looks eye-catching, but it distorts values and has no way to show a target.","Domain 4: Chart selection",["3D pie charts distort proportions and do not show a target for comparison.","A word cloud sizes words by frequency and cannot show revenue against a target.",null,"Raw transactions do not summarize progress toward the target and take far too much space."],2],["x41",0,4,"A support manager wants to see which hours of which weekdays get the most tickets, across 7 days and 24 hours. Which visual shows this pattern BEST?",["A pie chart of tickets by weekday","A heat map with weekdays as rows and hours as columns","A single KPI card showing total tickets","A histogram of ticket IDs"],1,"A heat map shades each day-and-hour cell by volume, so busy periods stand out at a glance. A pie chart by weekday is tempting, but it drops the hourly dimension.","Domain 4: Chart selection",["A pie of weekdays loses the hour-by-hour detail the manager needs.",null,"One total hides all the variation by day and hour.","Ticket IDs are identifiers, so their distribution says nothing about busy times."],1],["x42",0,4,"Finance wants to show how last year's operating profit of 2.0M became this year's 2.6M, step by step through higher sales, higher costs and a one-time gain. Which chart fits BEST?",["A donut chart","A scatter plot","A stacked area chart","A waterfall chart"],3,"A waterfall chart starts at one total and shows each positive or negative contribution as a floating bar until it reaches the ending total. A stacked area chart is tempting for change over time, but it cannot show a bridge of increases and decreases.","Domain 4: Chart selection",["A donut chart shows parts of one whole, not a sequence of increases and decreases between two totals.","A scatter plot shows the relationship between two numeric variables, not a step-by-step bridge.","A stacked area chart shows composition over time and cannot clearly separate positive and negative steps.",null],2],["x43",0,4,"One sales dashboard is shared with all regional managers, and each manager must see only their own region's data. What is the BEST way to implement this?",["Set up row-level security based on each user's identity","Publish a separate copy of the dashboard for every region","Tell managers to use the Region filter and not look at other regions","Hide the Region column so managers cannot tell which data is whose"],0,"Row-level security filters data automatically according to who is signed in, enforcing access with one shared dashboard. Separate copies also restrict data, but they are hard to maintain and easy to share incorrectly.","Domain 4: Dashboard security",[null,"Separate copies multiply maintenance work and can drift out of sync, while still relying on who gets which link.","A filter the user controls is a convenience, not an access control, so other regions stay visible.","Hiding a column does not restrict rows, so every manager would still see all regions' data."],3],["x44",0,4,"An analyst built a detailed statistical model of churn and must now present the results to the sales team, who are not technical. What is the BEST approach?",["Walk through the full model code line by line","Lead with key findings and actions in plain language and simple visuals","Show the complete correlation matrix of all 60 variables","Send the raw dataset so the team can explore it and draw its own conclusions"],1,"Tailoring to the audience means leading with the insights and actions that matter to them, using plain language and simple charts. Showing the full technical detail can feel thorough, but it obscures the message for non-technical readers.","Domain 4: Communicating with audiences",["Reading code to a non-technical audience buries the findings they need in details they cannot use.",null,"A large correlation matrix overwhelms a non-technical audience and hides the main message.","Raw data shifts the analysis work to people who lack the tools and time to do it."],2],["x45",0,4,"Users want to click on a yearly revenue bar and see that same chart break down into quarters, then into months, within the same visual. Which feature provides this?",["A static image export of the chart","A tooltip that shows the exact yearly value","Drill-down through a date hierarchy","A slicer that filters to a single product"],2,"Drill-down navigates down a hierarchy, such as year to quarter to month, within the same visual. A tooltip is tempting because it reveals details on hover, but it does not change the chart's level.","Domain 4: Dashboard interactivity",["A static image cannot respond to clicks or reveal lower levels of detail.","A tooltip shows more information about the same point but does not change the level of detail shown.",null,"A product slicer filters by product and does not move between year, quarter and month."],2],["x46",0,4,"The operations director wants the same weekly performance report in her inbox every Monday at 7 a.m. without asking anyone. What should the analyst set up?",["A reminder for the analyst to email the report manually each Monday","A new dashboard page that she must remember to open","A one-time export of this week's report","A scheduled report subscription that delivers it automatically"],3,"A scheduled subscription runs and delivers the report on a fixed recurrence with no manual steps. A calendar reminder to send it by hand seems simple, but it is fragile and depends on one person.","Domain 4: Report delivery",["Manual sending depends on the analyst being available and is easy to forget.","A dashboard page still requires her to go and look, which is not what she asked for.","A one-time export covers only one week and would need to be repeated by hand.",null],1],["x47",0,4,"A table on a dashboard lists 40 products with their current sales. Managers also want to see each product's 12-month trend without leaving the table. What should be added?",["A sparkline in each row","A separate pie chart for each product","A single large line chart showing all 40 products","A color legend explaining the table"],0,"Sparklines are tiny, axis-free line charts that fit inside a table row to show the shape of a trend. One large combined line chart is tempting, but 40 overlapping series are hard to read.","Domain 4: Visualization design",[null,"Forty pie charts take a lot of space and pie charts cannot show a trend over time anyway.","Forty overlapping lines on one chart would be unreadable and take readers away from the table.","A legend explains colors but adds no trend information to the rows."],2],["x48",0,4,"A map colors each store by profit margin, which ranges from −15% to +20%, and readers must quickly tell losses from gains. Which color scheme is MOST appropriate?",["A sequential palette from light blue to dark blue","A diverging palette with a neutral midpoint at 0%","A random categorical palette with a different color per store","A single color at full saturation for every store"],1,"A diverging palette uses two contrasting hues around a meaningful midpoint, here zero, so negative and positive values are instantly distinct. A sequential palette is tempting for numeric data, but it does not emphasize the midpoint.","Domain 4: Color in visualization",["A one-hue sequential palette shows magnitude but makes the break between loss and gain hard to see.",null,"Categorical colors show identity, not order or magnitude, so margins cannot be compared.","One color for every store conveys no information about margin at all."],3],["x49",0,4,"Business users keep sending the analyst small requests for new views of the same sales data. Management wants them to answer these questions themselves while still using trusted numbers. What is the BEST approach?",["Give every user direct write access to the production database","Email everyone a full copy of the raw sales tables each week","Provide self-service BI on a certified, governed dataset","Ask users to submit all requests in writing so they can be prioritized"],2,"Self-service BI built on a certified dataset lets users explore freely while metrics and definitions stay consistent. A formal request queue is tempting for control, but it keeps the analyst as the bottleneck.","Domain 4: Self-service reporting",["Write access to production risks accidental changes and gives no shared, trusted definitions.","Raw copies spread uncontrolled data and lead to many versions of the numbers.",null,"A request queue may help prioritization, but users still cannot answer their own questions."],2],["x50",0,4,"Readers of a new executive dashboard keep asking what 'Net Active Users' means and which data it uses. What should the analyst add to the report package?",["Add more decimal places to every metric so the values look precise","A larger company logo in the header","An animated introduction slide","A data dictionary with each metric's definition, source and logic"],3,"A data dictionary documents each metric's business definition, source and logic, so readers interpret it consistently. More precision is tempting as a quality signal, but it does not answer what the metric means.","Domain 4: Report documentation",["Extra decimal places add precision but do not explain what a metric means.","Branding elements do not define metrics or their sources.","Animations add visual interest but not the definitions the readers are asking for.",null],3],["x51",0,5,"Customer emails from 2019 are due to be deleted under the retention schedule, but legal counsel has just notified the team of pending litigation involving those customers. What should happen?",["Delete the records on schedule because the retention policy requires it","Move the records to a personal drive until the case ends","Anonymize the records and then delete the originals","Apply a legal hold and suspend deletion until it is released"],3,"A legal hold overrides the normal retention schedule and preserves relevant records until counsel releases it. Following the retention schedule is tempting because it is policy, but the hold takes precedence.","Domain 5: Retention and legal hold",["Destroying records relevant to pending litigation can be treated as spoliation of evidence.","Moving records to personal storage breaks security and chain-of-custody controls.","Altering or deleting originals under litigation also destroys potential evidence.",null],3],["x52",0,5,"A new analyst joins the finance reporting team. How should access to data sources be granted?",["Copy the permissions of the most senior person on the team","Assign the finance analyst role, granting only what the job requires","Grant administrator access so the analyst is never blocked","Share another analyst's login until the analyst's own account is ready"],1,"Role-based access that grants only what the job requires applies least privilege and is easy to review. Copying a senior person's access is a common shortcut, but it usually grants far more than needed.","Domain 5: Access control",["A senior colleague's permissions usually exceed what a new analyst needs, which violates least privilege.",null,"Administrator rights far exceed the job's needs and increase the damage from mistakes or misuse.","Shared credentials break accountability, since actions cannot be traced to the right person."],1],["x53",0,5,"A company's published price list is posted on its website for anyone to read. Under a typical data classification scheme, how should it be labeled?",["Restricted","Confidential","Public","Internal"],2,"Information already approved for release to anyone is classified as public. Internal is the tempting choice for company-owned information, but it implies the data should not be shared outside.","Domain 5: Data classification",["Restricted is the most sensitive tier, used for data whose exposure would cause serious harm.","Confidential data is limited to specific authorized people, which does not fit openly posted prices.",null,"Internal data is for employees only and is not meant to be published to everyone."],1],["x54",0,5,"A daily inventory dashboard is accurate, but the underlying data is loaded three days late, so managers reorder stock too late. Which data quality dimension is the problem?",["Timeliness","Validity","Uniqueness","Accuracy"],0,"Timeliness measures whether data is available when it is needed; correct data that arrives three days late fails that test. Accuracy is the tempting answer, but the figures themselves are right.","Domain 5: Data quality dimensions",[null,"Validity concerns whether values follow the expected format and rules, which they do here.","Uniqueness concerns duplicate records, which are not the issue described.","The values are correct, so accuracy is fine; they simply arrive too late to be useful."],2],["x55",0,5,"The sales system reports 1,204 orders for March, while the warehouse reports 1,187 orders for the same month using the same definition. Which data quality dimension is being violated?",["Completeness of the customer address field","Consistency between the two systems","Validity of the date format","Integrity of the report's color scheme"],1,"Consistency means the same data agrees across systems; different counts for the same month and definition show an inconsistency to reconcile. Completeness is tempting, since missing orders could be the cause, but the dimension being flagged is cross-system agreement.","Domain 5: Data quality dimensions",["Address completeness concerns blank fields, which does not explain two systems disagreeing on order counts.",null,"Nothing suggests invalid date formats; the issue is two systems reporting different counts.","Report colors are a presentation choice and are not a data quality dimension."],2],["x56",0,5,"A report pipeline copies customer data from an on-premises database to a cloud warehouse over the internet. Which control protects the data while it is being transferred?",["Encryption at rest on the warehouse disks","A longer data retention period","Row-level security on the dashboard","Encryption in transit, such as TLS"],3,"Encryption in transit, typically TLS, protects data as it crosses the network between systems. Encryption at rest is the tempting answer, but it applies to stored data, not the transfer.","Domain 5: Data protection",["Encryption at rest protects stored data, not data moving across the network.","Retention controls how long data is kept, not how it is protected during transfer.","Row-level security limits which rows users see in reports, not data moving between systems.",null],1],["x57",0,5,"An analytics team will store and analyze payment card numbers from an online checkout. Which standard sets the security requirements for handling this data?",["HIPAA","FERPA","PCI DSS","ISO 8601"],2,"PCI DSS is the payment card industry's security standard for any organization that stores, processes or transmits cardholder data. HIPAA is the tempting distractor because it is a well-known data protection rule, but it covers health data.","Domain 5: Compliance",["HIPAA protects health information in the United States, not payment card data.","FERPA protects student education records, not payment card data.",null,"ISO 8601 is a date and time format standard, not a security requirement for card data."],2],["x58",0,5,"In a data governance program, who is MOST responsible for maintaining business definitions, quality rules and metadata for the customer domain on a day-to-day basis?",["The data steward","The chief executive officer","The database administrator","An external auditor"],0,"Data stewards handle the daily care of a data domain, including definitions, quality rules and metadata, on behalf of the data owner. The DBA is tempting because they work closely with data, but their role is technical custody.","Domain 5: Governance roles",[null,"The CEO sponsors governance at the top but does not maintain domain definitions day to day.","The DBA manages the technical platform, such as backups and performance, rather than business definitions.","An auditor independently reviews controls and does not maintain definitions or quality rules."],2],["x59",0,5,"A public report shows employee counts by department, job level and gender. One cell shows exactly one female director in a small department. What should the analyst do before publishing?",["Publish as is, because counts are aggregated and contain no names","Replace the department name with its internal code number","Round every count up to the next even number","Suppress or combine small cells so no one can be identified"],3,"Small-cell suppression or combining categories prevents aggregate tables from revealing information about identifiable individuals. Publishing as is is tempting because the data is aggregated, but very small counts can still point to one person.","Domain 5: Privacy in reporting",["A count of one can identify a specific person, so aggregation alone does not protect privacy here.","Anyone familiar with the organization can map a code back to the department, so the person remains identifiable.","Rounding small counts inconsistently can still reveal individuals and distorts the totals.",null],3],["x60",0,5,"Customers gave their phone numbers only to receive delivery updates. The marketing team now wants to use the same numbers for promotional text campaigns. What is the MAIN governance concern?",["The phone numbers might be in the wrong data type","Reusing data beyond its original purpose without consent","Text campaigns cost more than email campaigns","The delivery system might run out of storage space for the extra messages"],1,"Purpose limitation means personal data collected for one purpose should not be reused for another without a lawful basis such as consent. Cost is tempting as a practical concern, but the core issue is permission to use the data.","Domain 5: Privacy principles",["Data type issues are a technical detail and do not address whether the use is permitted.",null,"Campaign cost is a business consideration, not a governance or privacy concern.","Storage capacity is unrelated to whether marketing is allowed to use the data."],3]]);
